Hi Richard,

First, sorry to not having replied before, and many thanks for your
help.

The SCSI card was indeed not detected by the OS, so I had to introduce
it the Kernel. I did this by booting from genvmunix.

So, I first tried to boot from genvmunix, but it crashed because it
changed the device numbers, so couldn't mount disk partitions :-(

Then, I booted from genvmunix in SINGLE user mode so I can do
something. From there I could see that what was before /dev/rz16 was
now /dev/rz24 and /dev/rz17 was now /dev/rz25. In fact it changed the
SCSI bus number (hard disks were now detected on BUS 3 and not on BUS 2
like before).
Then, I changed the ADVFS domain references to link to the new disk
devices, created the new tape device, rebuilt the kernel, and rebooted.

Finally it worked ! (tape detected, backup test ok).
